From a69882d04d6a34221c4a2a830d199cacf3e8e14b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Horizon authors <>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:55:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Draw the battle effects at the frame rate the game renders at

The EFE engine moves its effects once per 20 Hz tick, so at 30 and 60 fps every pass of a tick drew the same effect pose while the camera and the models moved on. Each draw sub-opcode of a render script now goes through modSmoothCall (include/dw/mod_efe_smooth.h, in all three battle engines): on a pass that shows a fraction of the tick it draws its positions, scales, colours, angles, screen coordinates and the instance transform of the model draws between the previous tick's values and its own. The particle emitters draw each particle back along its last step.

Nothing persistent is written: operands are rewritten on the script stack (pointer operands swapped for local copies) and the instance transform is put back after the draw, so ticks, hits, damage and sounds keep their timing, and at 20 fps (or with interpolation off) nothing changes.

A draw is matched to the one of the previous tick by slot, sub-effect and order in its render script, plus a hash of its opcode, script, instance, pointer operands and identifiers (model, sprite cell, flags), and its instance must be exactly one frame further. Spawns, flipbook switches, restarted sub-effects, camera cuts and any value that jumps more than its kind allows in one tick draw their own pose; values that already differ between the passes of a tick (read from the interpolated bones or camera) are taken as they are. Angles take the short way round.

The two per-tick logs (4 KB each) are overlay bss; battle_effect.c's .bss is placed in BTL (it was not listed). The executable does not change.
